00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.039
So Google Cloud,
we've been trying

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:12.830
to solve data problems
for a long time.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:14.420
It started in 2004.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:17.120
We came out with one of the
original MapReduce papers

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.100
that got turned-- a
lot of those elements

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.700
got put into the
original Apache Hadoop.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:26.240
About a year later we released
a paper about Bigtable.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.430
And that had a lot
[INAUDIBLE] bases.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.160
And a lot of that
got put into HBase.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.340
More recently, we've even open
sourced things with machine

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.500
learning code in TensorFlow.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.230
Now about 10 years
back, we started

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:42.880
to actually take a lot of that
infrastructure that was built--

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.320
research papers,
things that were battle

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.200
tested on Gmail and maps--

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.270
and we started to put
those out as products

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.100
that any company--
regardless of if you're

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.680
a startup in a garage
or a large enterprise--

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.740
could go, use, and start to use
the same technology that Google

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.120
was using.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:00.620
And so we took all
of this knowledge

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:03.710
from research and open source
and running our own cloud

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:06.395
platform going all
the way back to 2004

00:03:06.395 --> 00:03:08.270
with that first MapReduce
paper, we took that

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.730
and we put it into
Cloud Dataproc.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.605
And if you're not familiar
with what that is,

00:03:12.605 --> 00:03:16.820
that is Google Cloud Platform
fully managed Apache Spark

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:19.820
and Apache Hadoop service.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.640
I'm saying Hadoop and
Spark, but really, this

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.430
is an engine for running
lots of open source software

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:27.193
within that ecosystem.

00:03:27.193 --> 00:03:28.610
And we're going
to give you things

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:31.260
like customizable machine types.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.730
So let's say you have a batch
of machine learning jobs.

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:36.560
Those could live
on a cluster that's

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.860
got very compute
intensive specifications.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:44.030
At the same time, you could
have a lot of BI applications

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.730
and dashboards and
interactive SQL

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.280
that's running on a cluster
that's a very memory heavy.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.740
The two never have to
contend for resources,

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:54.820
but both could still be reading
and writing from the same data

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:58.600
set in cloud storage.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:02.670
And we also offer a lot of
tools and features specifically

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:06.870
designed to help you with
those kinds of architectures,

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:12.512
as well as ephemeral Hadoop and
Spark architectures in general.

00:04:12.512 --> 00:04:13.970
We're also going
to try to give you

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:18.680
tight integration back to the
rest of Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:22.610
The idea with Cloud Dataproc is
that we're not just giving you

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:24.950
yet another cluster to manage.

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:27.973
We're actually going to let you
run your open source software,

00:04:27.973 --> 00:04:30.140
but in a way that will
actually modernize your stack

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:33.090
at the same time.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:34.820
We also give you a
lot of flexibility

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.440
when running Dataproc.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:40.680
We exposed a lot of those
knobs that you can turn.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.995
Now we try to put some same
default on those knobs,

00:04:42.995 --> 00:04:44.620
so if you don't want
to deal with them,

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:46.330
you can get started very easily.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:48.930
But if you do want to
start tuning the knobs,

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:51.593
you have the ability to
get in there and do that.

00:04:51.593 --> 00:04:53.010
But that also means
that customers

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:55.720
are using Cloud Dataproc
in a few different ways.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.380
So a couple of the common models
that we see customers using

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.720
Cloud Dataproc
with is first off,

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.020
what we call the job
scoped cluster model.

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:07.040
And this has been
really effective

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.250
for Cloud Dataproc customers
with batch processing and ETL

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.050
workloads.

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:16.730
Essentially in this mode, you
can have a single command that

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:19.670
sends a graph of jobs
to Cloud Dataproc,

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:23.720
we will spin up a right sized
cluster, run those jobs,

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:27.050
then take down the cluster,
while keeping everything logged

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:29.390
into Stackdriver.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:33.420
And this is really
easy on Cloud Dataproc,

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.400
really effective,
because we can usually

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:40.740
spin up a fully loaded Hadoop
cluster in around 90 seconds.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.530
So when we only have about
a minute and a half overhead

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:45.750
to get yourself a fully
loaded Hadoop cluster,

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:48.570
you really can start to
think about jobs and clusters

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.700
as a single entity.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:54.120
And Cloud Dataproc has a lot
of features like the Jobs API,

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.910
workflow templates, and
scheduled cluster deletion

00:05:56.910 --> 00:06:01.910
that can help with this
model of job scope clusters.

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:06.230
But we also have customers
that have scenarios for long

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:08.330
running Dataproc clusters.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:12.080
And some of those situations
include a shared interactive

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:15.350
or ad hoc analysis with
web based notebooks

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:19.250
like Jupiter or Zeppelin, or
BI dashboarding applications

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:21.320
based on things like Druid.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:23.840
And we have a lot of features
here that help as well.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:26.962
Things like our auto scaler
or high availability mode.

00:06:26.962 --> 00:06:28.670
And if you want to
learn more, I actually

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:32.440
did a top 10 tips for
longstanding Dataproc clusters

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:33.500
a couple months back.

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:35.042
There's a webinar
associated with it.

00:06:35.042 --> 00:06:36.625
So there's lots of
materials out there

00:06:36.625 --> 00:06:39.170
if you want to learn more about
how to use longstanding Cloud

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:41.580
Dataproc clusters.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.500
But regardless of the
model that you choose,

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:49.760
our goal is to change
open source software

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:51.650
to be cloud native.

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:53.600
And so this means
making features

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:58.220
that are going to be fast,
easy, and cost effective.

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:02.330
Last year alone, Cloud Dataproc
launched more than 30 features.

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:04.850
So this is still an area
that Google Cloud is really

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.780
investing in.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.000
And I'm calling out
cost effective here.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.730
And we actually had
some external validation

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:15.270
that we're building from
cost effective features.

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:18.570
We worked with an analyst firm
ESG, who came back and told us

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:23.160
that compared to an on prem
self-managed Hadoop cluster,

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:26.560
Cloud Dataproc is about
60% less expensive.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:30.120
And even compared to some of
our competitors like Amazon

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:33.750
EMR in the cloud space,
we still came in about 32%

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:35.490
less expensive in
terms of total cost

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:38.250
of ownership for these
Cloud Dataproc clusters.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:45.330
And so cost is an
obvious optimization.

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:47.910
But most customers that
are coming to Blake and I,

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:50.380
it's not cost that's
the main driver.

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:52.410
They are really coming
to us because of a desire

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.200
to make better data
driven decisions.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.170
And this is usually the
appeal of the cloud migrations

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:01.670
much more so than
just cost alone.

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:03.530
And the key to
making that happen

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:06.950
is really unlocking all of
the data that is trapped

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:09.770
in your HDFS clusters today.

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:12.270
And so I'm actually going to
do a full section on converting

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:15.910
HDFS to Google Cloud
Storage, because this

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:19.090
is really imperative
to achieving

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:21.530
cloud freedom for a cluster.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:25.300
This is what really severs
that dependency between storage

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:26.560
and compute.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:29.870
Now luckily, this is in code,
in the Hadoop code itself.

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:31.870
It's often one of the
easiest changes to make.

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:34.870
Usually, it's just as
simple as a prefix change.

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:37.600
But the bottom line is
that you can't really

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:42.669
scale HDFS because that storage
is still tied to compute.

00:08:42.669 --> 00:08:46.240
So you're taking a very
pricey resource of compute,

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:48.790
and tying yourself to a
relatively inexpensive resource

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:50.410
of storage.

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:55.420
Not only that, but you're also
siloing all of your HDFS data

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:58.078
to a single cluster
instead of exposing it

00:08:58.078 --> 00:08:59.620
to all of the
different possibilities

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:03.010
that a cloud provider
like GCP could offer.

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:05.772
Now there are some reasons to
still use HDFS in the cloud,

00:09:05.772 --> 00:09:06.730
especially on Dataproc.

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:09.260
You might have some
local storage devices.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:13.780
So you can do things like
scratch space and shuffle data,

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:15.640
or like, an LLAP cache.

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:19.090
But the idea is that's all
pretty much temporary data.

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:21.070
You want everything that
you plan to maintain

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:23.760
to be written to cloud storage.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.555
And the reason why you can do
this file system substitution,

00:09:27.555 --> 00:09:30.840
it's because Hadoop has this
abstract notion of a file

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:32.250
system.

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:35.930
And HDFS, that's just
one implementation.

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:38.850
There's a Java abstract
class fs.file system.

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:42.540
And that can call into
various replacements for HDFS.

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:45.150
All MapReduce or
Spark really care

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:48.340
about is getting blocks of data.

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:52.800
And so this idea, this concept,
it provided a lot of advantages

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:56.580
when HDFS was first developed,
and it still does now as well,

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:58.013
as we kind of move to the cloud.

00:09:58.013 --> 00:09:59.430
And I don't have
time to deep dive

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.335
on everything in this slide.

00:10:01.335 --> 00:10:03.210
I did release an article
a couple months back

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:06.480
if you care about comparing
cloud storage directly

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.180
to HDFS that does a deep
dive on a lot of this

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:12.210
and does a full comparison.

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:14.520
But the too long
didn't read version

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:19.050
of that is that you
can pretty much replace

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:23.060
most of your Hadoop jobs that
are in HDFS with cloud storage.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:25.320
And that does not mean
that you pull the data

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:27.360
from cloud storage into HDFS.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:31.140
It means that your Hadoop and
Spark jobs can read directly

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:35.510
from cloud storage just as they
can HDFS over our Google Cloud

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:37.260
Storage connector,
which is an open source

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:38.510
connector for doing just that.

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:42.930
There's also a couple
differentiators

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:45.390
that you should be aware
of with Cloud Storage

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:47.980
versus other cloud providers.

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:50.910
So in particular,
our Cloud Storage,

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:53.880
we're going to offer a
strong global consistency

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:56.460
for a lot of those operations
that you care a lot

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:58.278
about with Hadoop and Spark.

00:10:58.278 --> 00:10:59.820
So you don't have
to be afraid to use

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:01.800
Cloud Storage as a destination.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:05.280
If you've worked with
objects stores on other cloud

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.070
providers, you may
have had to introduce

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:09.690
some clugy workarounds with
like, a NoSQL database that

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:13.500
sits in the way to make up for
their eventual consistency,

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:16.290
but you don't have to
do that on Google Cloud.

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:17.640
I was working with one customer.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:19.932
And they said they were trying
to move to another cloud

00:11:19.932 --> 00:11:21.220
provider for over a year.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:22.470
And they couldn't get it done.

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:25.260
There's too much conflicts
and all the different

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:26.820
writes they were doing.

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:29.010
They tried to do a POC.

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:31.310
Before giving up, they
tried to do a POC with us.

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:33.030
And in three weeks,
they were able to get

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:35.233
done what they couldn't
get done in a year trying

00:11:35.233 --> 00:11:36.900
to get on another
cloud provider because

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:39.930
of this strong consistency.

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:43.450
In addition, our renames
are metadata operations.

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:46.140
So what that means is a lot
of other cloud providers--

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:50.040
if you were to do a Hadoop
move, all of the data associated

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:53.160
with that Hadoop move, that's
going to actually get copied

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:55.530
to a new part of object store.

00:11:55.530 --> 00:11:57.780
And then you have to wait
for that to happen, and then

00:11:57.780 --> 00:11:59.490
wait for all of the
old data to also

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:02.850
get deleted before you get a
return from your Hadoop move.

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:05.970
For us, all of that happens
as a metadata operation.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:10.160
You're not actually going to
have to move the data around.

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:11.750
Another differentiator
that we have

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:14.818
is that across
Google Cloud Storage,

00:12:14.818 --> 00:12:16.610
we're going to give
you a different storage

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:19.130
classes for cost optimization.

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:21.230
But you're actually going
to get a consistent API

00:12:21.230 --> 00:12:23.810
across our storage portfolio.

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:26.630
So you don't actually have to
change your Hadoop or Spark

00:12:26.630 --> 00:12:30.020
code as you move across
these storage classes.

00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:32.330
You can get direct
access to the archive.

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:34.640
You don't have to go
to a different service,

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:39.530
wait hours for a restore,
and then rerun your code.

00:12:39.530 --> 00:12:40.910
You can just go.

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:43.970
Even between our regional
and nearline storage classes,

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:47.420
you can even get the same less
than a millisecond latency

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:49.490
on first byte reads.

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:51.290
So in the context of
Hadoop, just the way

00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:54.380
to think about our storage
classes, regional storage,

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:57.350
this is what you're going to use
for your interactive high Spark

00:12:57.350 --> 00:12:59.570
analysis or batch
jobs that might

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:02.160
occur more than once a month.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:04.360
Next, you're going to
have nearline storage.

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:06.930
And this is for batch
jobs that are really used

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:08.820
for reporting or aggregations.

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:10.740
The rule of thumb
here is data that you

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:13.530
expect to use about no
more than once a month

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:15.180
you'll put into nearline.

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:17.220
And then finally,
code line storage--

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:20.340
that's for your post process
data, things you really

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:22.170
don't expect to
use again, but you

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:24.660
want to keep around for
either compliance reasons

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:26.910
or just to have it just in case.

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:29.580
That can be moved to
cold line storage.

00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:31.710
Rule of thumb on
that one is data

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:34.680
you don't expect to touch
again more than once a year.

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:39.150
And so once you've
made the shift,

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.070
and you're able
to move your HDFS

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:43.560
data into Cloud
Storage, that opens up

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:46.090
a lot more possibilities of
things you can do with it.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:48.630
So if you have multiple
distributions of both Horton

00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:53.590
Works and Cloudera in
your environment today,

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:55.980
both of those can talk over
the GCS connector directly

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:58.200
to Cloud Storage,
while at the same time,

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:02.190
it opens you up to use a lot
more Google Cloud tooling.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:05.340
So you can start to incorporate
maybe Dataproc for some

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:07.470
of your batch
processing, or maybe you

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:11.760
can start to move some ad
hoc stuff into BigQuery

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:14.220
and access Cloud Storage
via external tables,

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:16.860
or even start to introduce
new streaming type

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:19.692
applications with Dataflow on
top of all the same data sets.

00:14:19.692 --> 00:14:21.150
And you can be sure
that everything

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:24.130
is strongly consistent.

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:27.090
So if you want to
make the move, you

00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:30.210
can move an entire
petabyte of data

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:34.975
from HDFS to Cloud Storage
in about three steps.

00:14:34.975 --> 00:14:37.350
What you would do is you would
come into the Google Cloud

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:41.500
Console and request
a Transfer Appliance.

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:43.300
We would then ship
you a storage device.

00:14:43.300 --> 00:14:46.900
It would look something like
this box on the screen here.

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:49.830
You would put it into an
NFS share capture mode,

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:53.275
mount those NFS shares
to each of your data

00:14:53.275 --> 00:14:54.880
nodes in your Hadoop cluster.

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:57.610
And then it's just as simple
as running the state or distcp

00:14:57.610 --> 00:14:58.300
Hadoop command.

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:00.610
And you'll copy all that
data over in parallel.

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:02.710
At that point, you just
ship us the device back,

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:06.210
and we'll go ahead and load
that into Cloud Storage for you.

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:08.045
Now if you don't have
a petabyte of data

00:15:08.045 --> 00:15:09.420
that you want to
move right away,

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:12.990
but you just want to start
with some proof of concept,

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:15.630
or maybe a data set size
that can move comfortably

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:18.630
across a VPN connection,
you can simply

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:20.940
install the Google
Cloud Storage connector

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:25.260
on an existing on
prem Hadoop cluster,

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:30.370
and then copy the data
over with a distcp.

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:33.740
I think, as far as I know, we're
the only cloud provider that

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:37.790
supports not only copying
of the data over distcp,

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:41.700
but copying of the metadata
attribute from HDFS as well.

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:44.120
So if you don't know what
I'm talking about when

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:46.640
I say metadata attributes,
what this means is these

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:48.530
are a file system
feature that are

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:50.330
going to allow a
user applications

00:15:50.330 --> 00:15:54.560
to associate additional metadata
for a file or a directory.

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:56.810
Oftentimes, you'll see
applications do this for things

00:15:56.810 --> 00:15:59.670
like maybe what
encoding was used.

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:02.940
But when I ran across this, I
was working with a large bank

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:05.450
and they wanted to do
a full HDFS backup,

00:16:05.450 --> 00:16:08.510
keep everything encrypted as
is, just get it copied over

00:16:08.510 --> 00:16:10.070
to Cloud Storage.

00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:14.390
And what they were doing is
they had an on prem HSM that

00:16:14.390 --> 00:16:17.883
was storing the key locations
in the metadata attributes.

00:16:17.883 --> 00:16:19.800
And so when they're
comparing cloud providers,

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:21.217
they were looking
and saying, well

00:16:21.217 --> 00:16:22.880
if I go to some
other folks, I would

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:25.130
have to figure out a new
encryption scheme or decrypt

00:16:25.130 --> 00:16:26.850
everything before I moved over.

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:28.340
But with us, it
was just as simple

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:31.350
as running that
distcp-px command.

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:33.390
The px stands for
preserve attributes.

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:35.870
And we we're able to
take all of both the HDFS

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:38.040
data and the
metadata attributes,

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:41.390
and they could use
it as their backup.

00:16:41.390 --> 00:16:45.230
But once that data is available
in Google Cloud Storage,

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:47.750
now that's going to let
you take a really hard look

00:16:47.750 --> 00:16:50.600
at your existing
Hadoop cluster and see

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:54.730
what workloads might make
sense to move to Google Cloud.

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:57.580
A common workload target
that I'll start with

00:16:57.580 --> 00:17:00.010
is they taken on prem
Hadoop cluster that might

00:17:00.010 --> 00:17:03.820
pretty monolithic and sharing
both ad hoc analysis and batch

00:17:03.820 --> 00:17:04.720
reporting.

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:06.807
So they have business
user that are in there.

00:17:06.807 --> 00:17:09.099
And they're struggling to
get their SQL queries to run,

00:17:09.099 --> 00:17:10.569
because over the last
few years, people

00:17:10.569 --> 00:17:12.920
have scheduled a bunch of jobs,
had a bunch of stuff in batch.

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:14.530
They're just now
piling up and they're

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:16.095
contending for resources.

00:17:16.095 --> 00:17:17.470
So it's a easy
way to get started

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:20.260
in the cloud is they just start
to offload a lot of that batch

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:23.140
stuff to Cloud Dataproc,
which frees up there

00:17:23.140 --> 00:17:27.970
on prem resources for
the business analyst.

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:31.690
But Cloud Dataproc it might
not always be the best choice,

00:17:31.690 --> 00:17:33.890
depending on your goals.

00:17:33.890 --> 00:17:37.480
For instance, in a lot
of ad hoc Hive SQL,

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:40.540
that migrates really well to a
BigQuery, our serverless data

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:41.868
warehouse.

00:17:41.868 --> 00:17:43.660
Also, there might be
streaming applications

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:46.490
that fit better with Dataflow.

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:51.260
But regardless of your
tool or application,

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:53.000
having that data
in Cloud Storage,

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:55.250
that's what's going to
give you the freedom

00:17:55.250 --> 00:17:57.410
to modernize your
environment and actually

00:17:57.410 --> 00:17:59.990
start to pick the
right tool for the job

00:17:59.990 --> 00:18:02.450
instead of just being
limited to the tools

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:06.410
and the compute resources that
are on that single HDFS storage

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:08.740
cluster.

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:11.500
Now we aren't doing this alone.

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:14.460
We have a lot of partners
in this space that can help.

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:16.980
We are partnered with all the
traditional Hadoop vendors

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:19.200
to help you with
data processing.

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:21.450
We have partners that can
help with data ingestion

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:23.160
and movement, and
service partners

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:25.410
that can actually be
your hands-on keyboard

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:27.390
to actually get you migrated.

00:18:27.390 --> 00:18:30.360
In addition, we have some new
partnerships for some niche

00:18:30.360 --> 00:18:31.830
solution providers.

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:34.800
They can bring a unified kind of
experience to Hadoop and Spark.

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:37.080
And you're going to hear
more about that in our data

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:40.150
analytics keynote tomorrow.

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:43.480
But like I said, the
big three on prem Hadoop

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:46.420
platforms in this space, they
all support the GCS connector

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:47.870
today.

00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:52.040
So you can move your data
between HDFS and Cloud Storage,

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:55.520
or you can even process
Cloud Storage data directly

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:59.070
from these platforms.

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:01.260
We're also partnered with
folks like Informatica

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:04.740
among others who can support
very complex enterprise data

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:06.120
workflows.

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:08.730
And they really help because
they integrate deeply

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:10.860
with GCP services,
but also you're

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:16.090
on prem scheduling
systems today.

00:19:16.090 --> 00:19:18.850
And finally, again, we
have migration partners

00:19:18.850 --> 00:19:21.520
that actually can
get this stuff moved.

00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:24.070
We have a lot longer
list than just these,

00:19:24.070 --> 00:19:26.470
but these are the
partners that we know,

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:28.810
that at least I know have
a lot of proof points

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:32.110
for successfully migrating
customers from on prem Hadoop

00:19:32.110 --> 00:19:35.150
to GCP relatively quickly.

00:19:35.150 --> 00:19:37.000
So with this, I'm
going to hand this off

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:39.673
to Blake, who's going
to talk and give us

00:19:39.673 --> 00:19:41.590
some firsthand experience
on how he's actually

00:19:41.590 --> 00:19:44.230
migrated some really massive
Hadoop footprints to the Cloud.

00:19:44.230 --> 00:19:46.390
BLAKE DUBOIS: Thanks, Chris.

00:19:46.390 --> 00:19:47.590
Hi, everyone.

00:19:47.590 --> 00:19:50.370
So first, we're going to talk
about some reference cases.

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:52.120
We tend to see a
few uber-patterns

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:54.820
that people follow in their
move to Google Cloud Platform

00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:56.195
in terms of Hadoop and Spark.

00:19:56.195 --> 00:19:58.570
And we're going to tell that
through a couple of customer

00:19:58.570 --> 00:20:00.430
stories.

00:20:00.430 --> 00:20:02.230
So first off, with
the uber-patterns,

00:20:02.230 --> 00:20:04.670
we tend to see three
primary use cases.

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:06.430
So the first is lift and shift.

00:20:06.430 --> 00:20:07.930
These are customers
that are looking

00:20:07.930 --> 00:20:10.750
to lift and move to
GCP because they're

00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:15.440
having pain around scaling,
elasticity, globalization, et

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:15.940
cetera.

00:20:15.940 --> 00:20:19.540
And they sometimes want
to move very quickly,

00:20:19.540 --> 00:20:21.910
and sometimes they want
to move without risk.

00:20:21.910 --> 00:20:25.000
That can drive them to kind
of the lift and shift pattern.

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:27.910
Sometimes customers will
decouple, storage, and compute,

00:20:27.910 --> 00:20:29.330
and sometimes they will not.

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:32.186
And we'll talk a little
bit about that later.

00:20:32.186 --> 00:20:33.910
A customer example
we're going to go over

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:37.000
today is Twitter, which started
their Google Cloud Platform

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:40.180
journey about a year ago, and
has quite a few talks here

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:42.130
at Next '19.

00:20:42.130 --> 00:20:43.750
And the kind of
partner technologies

00:20:43.750 --> 00:20:45.670
we kind of run into
in this case are

00:20:45.670 --> 00:20:48.910
things like on premises Hadoop
distributions, Cloudera,

00:20:48.910 --> 00:20:51.730
Hortonworks, MapR,
as well as customers

00:20:51.730 --> 00:20:55.070
that use Apache Big Top
and customize themselves.

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:56.940
The key GCP services
that are used

00:20:56.940 --> 00:21:00.485
are GCE Google and Compute
Engine for raw compute,

00:21:00.485 --> 00:21:02.860
as well as Google Cloud Storage
that we kind of went over

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:03.970
today.

00:21:03.970 --> 00:21:06.940
Next is lift and
re-architect or modernize.

00:21:06.940 --> 00:21:12.910
These are customers-- they want
to kind of alleviate more than

00:21:12.910 --> 00:21:13.570
just--

00:21:13.570 --> 00:21:15.500
they want to realize more than
just the decoupling of storage

00:21:15.500 --> 00:21:17.530
and compute or the
elasticity of the cloud.

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:20.380
They want to reduce
operational overhead.

00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:21.850
They want auto scaling.

00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:24.400
They want to just
pay for the resources

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:28.750
that they need, use things
like auto scaling and et cetera

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:30.970
by using kind of dynamic
and ephemeral clusters.

00:21:30.970 --> 00:21:33.550
Kind of like Chris was telling
earlier with a pattern.

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:35.200
We're going to go
over a great customer

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:36.893
example, 3PM Solutions.

00:21:36.893 --> 00:21:39.310
And the key services that are
used here, on top of the two

00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:40.768
that we already
discussed, Dataproc

00:21:40.768 --> 00:21:42.610
for Managed Hadoop
and Spark Environment,

00:21:42.610 --> 00:21:46.960
as well as Composer, which is
our managed Apache Airflow.

00:21:46.960 --> 00:21:48.580
Next is transform
to cloud native.

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:52.510
So customers that are
looking to kind of really

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:57.220
transform how they're doing
batch stream processing,

00:21:57.220 --> 00:21:59.580
and adopt kind of like,
Cloud native products.

00:21:59.580 --> 00:22:02.200
You know, these are customers
that are usually operating

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:05.050
and at the high end
of the data scale.

00:22:05.050 --> 00:22:08.350
They're looking for advanced
things around Object Lifecycle

00:22:08.350 --> 00:22:09.520
Management.

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:13.735
They're looking to kind
of streamline their batch

00:22:13.735 --> 00:22:17.840
and streaming pipelines by using
a unified analytical engine,

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:19.840
which we'll go into
a little bit earlier.

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:22.040
And on top of just
using the technology

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:23.707
that we kind of talked
to today, they're

00:22:23.707 --> 00:22:27.190
also doing things like
ML Engine or BigQuery.

00:22:27.190 --> 00:22:31.090
They're doing ML or BigQuery.

00:22:31.090 --> 00:22:35.500
And I guess one last point here
is that this is not necessarily

00:22:35.500 --> 00:22:36.910
a sequential journey.

00:22:36.910 --> 00:22:39.130
Some customers targets
lift and shift.

00:22:39.130 --> 00:22:41.560
And then not only
do they migrate

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:43.848
to lift and re-architect
or transform cloud native,

00:22:43.848 --> 00:22:46.390
but because some customers kind
of make that jump immediately

00:22:46.390 --> 00:22:47.080
to cloud native.

00:22:47.080 --> 00:22:55.240
So your journey is dependent
upon the time, the risk

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:57.230
profile, and a lot
of other factors.

00:22:57.230 --> 00:23:01.068
And Google Cloud can help
you make the right choice.

00:23:01.068 --> 00:23:02.860
So the first pattern
we're going to go into

00:23:02.860 --> 00:23:04.730
is kind of lift and shift.

00:23:04.730 --> 00:23:06.550
So we see here is that
customers typically

00:23:06.550 --> 00:23:09.820
have a monolithic Hadoop cluster
that's running on premises.

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:11.980
We're co-locating
storage and compute.

00:23:11.980 --> 00:23:15.130
And across and
interconnect or VPN,

00:23:15.130 --> 00:23:19.362
we are moving data using distcp,
or in some cases, the transfer

00:23:19.362 --> 00:23:21.820
appliance that Chris talked
about earlier into Google Cloud

00:23:21.820 --> 00:23:23.470
Storage.

00:23:23.470 --> 00:23:26.050
That Google Cloud Storage can
be used in one of two ways.

00:23:26.050 --> 00:23:30.280
For clusters that
are being spun up

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:33.980
with the goal of being
decoupled from their storage,

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:37.090
the compute is created in
GCE with the distribution

00:23:37.090 --> 00:23:37.930
of your choice.

00:23:37.930 --> 00:23:40.930
Edge nodes are
added with the tools

00:23:40.930 --> 00:23:45.090
of your choice, things like
Jupiter Lab, Zeppelin, et

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:45.610
cetera.

00:23:45.610 --> 00:23:49.510
And they directly use the GCS
connector and the GS prefix

00:23:49.510 --> 00:23:53.210
to access the data
in cloud storage.

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:56.320
Four clusters are
also hosting data

00:23:56.320 --> 00:24:02.380
in on the GCE VM's
local disk for HDFS.

00:24:02.380 --> 00:24:04.930
Customers are looking to use
tools such as Impala, which

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:07.840
has limited connectivity
to Google Cloud

00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:10.342
Storage at this time
will then saturate

00:24:10.342 --> 00:24:12.675
the data in each shift that's
from Google Cloud Storage.

00:24:15.860 --> 00:24:18.240
So a customer that is
kind of doing this very,

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:19.640
very successfully-- we
have a lot of customers

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:20.690
actually, doing
this successfully

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:22.607
as a starting point and
moving into the cloud,

00:24:22.607 --> 00:24:25.640
but one of the
largest is Twitter.

00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:27.650
So you know, Twitter
has currently

00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:31.220
on premises in their
various Hadoop clusters,

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:35.000
around half a million
cores of Hadoop.

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:38.040
Some of these clusters are
as large as 12,500 nodes.

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:40.820
There's under 300 petabytes
of data under management.

00:24:40.820 --> 00:24:47.210
And these systems process over
1 trillion messages per day.

00:24:47.210 --> 00:24:51.380
The breakdown of the workloads
is kind of seen on top part

00:24:51.380 --> 00:24:53.240
in the image clashing colors.

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:57.380
But we see that processing and
production and batch processing

00:24:57.380 --> 00:25:00.620
using scouting is
about 30% real time,

00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:03.590
production processing is about
60% using quite a few tools,

00:25:03.590 --> 00:25:06.350
and ad hoc processing in
tools like Presto in Hive

00:25:06.350 --> 00:25:08.540
is about 10%.

00:25:08.540 --> 00:25:13.640
Twitter is actually quite
vocal with their journey

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:14.270
to the cloud.

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:16.880
And they're
continuing that trend

00:25:16.880 --> 00:25:20.540
by presenting multiple
talks at Google Next 2019.

00:25:20.540 --> 00:25:22.765
So we see here is
a couple of talks.

00:25:22.765 --> 00:25:24.140
They kind of
primarily break down

00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:28.983
into how is Twitter migrating
that much data into GDP.

00:25:28.983 --> 00:25:30.650
So if you're interested
in understanding

00:25:30.650 --> 00:25:36.200
how large their interconnects
are, how they use distcp, how

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:38.990
they verify the integrity
of the data, et cetera,

00:25:38.990 --> 00:25:41.900
the first talk is a
great one for you.

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:45.800
If you're looking to understand
how Twitter understands

00:25:45.800 --> 00:25:49.040
and implements Object lifecycle
management policies in Google

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:51.500
Cloud and basically extends
their on premises custom

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:54.880
tooling that they
do open source,

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:58.010
last talk on what
the architecture

00:25:58.010 --> 00:26:02.300
is for data storage and GCS and
how user identity management

00:26:02.300 --> 00:26:03.410
comes into play--

00:26:03.410 --> 00:26:05.870
great sessions.

00:26:05.870 --> 00:26:06.945
So the second talk is--

00:26:06.945 --> 00:26:08.320
I mean, sorry,
the second pattern

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:12.010
is lift and re-architecture
or modernize.

00:26:12.010 --> 00:26:14.140
And so now we're getting
into some of the things

00:26:14.140 --> 00:26:15.880
that Chris illustrated
the Dataproc is

00:26:15.880 --> 00:26:17.800
very capable of doing.

00:26:17.800 --> 00:26:21.100
What we see here is that
customers, once again,

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:23.110
are moving data into
Google Cloud Storage,

00:26:23.110 --> 00:26:25.240
either via transfer
of clients or GCP.

00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:28.180
But then it's really
unlocking the ability

00:26:28.180 --> 00:26:34.750
for us to bring very, very
defined resources to bear

00:26:34.750 --> 00:26:39.220
on that data, and that GCS
provides near unlimited ingress

00:26:39.220 --> 00:26:41.523
and egress bandwidth
to basically support

00:26:41.523 --> 00:26:42.940
all these analytical
engines using

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:44.540
the data at the same time.

00:26:44.540 --> 00:26:48.220
And so here on the left, we see
Cloud Composer or Manage Apache

00:26:48.220 --> 00:26:51.590
Airflow spinning up
job scope clusters.

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:54.670
This is traditionally done
for batch or ETL processes.

00:26:54.670 --> 00:26:57.340
You can do things
understand how much data

00:26:57.340 --> 00:27:00.880
you have to ETL, and
dynamically assign

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:03.460
a cluster with x
amount of nodes,

00:27:03.460 --> 00:27:06.860
spin that up, perform
the ETL batch process,

00:27:06.860 --> 00:27:07.990
and then tear it down.

00:27:07.990 --> 00:27:11.140
You're only paying for
exactly what you use.

00:27:11.140 --> 00:27:13.270
And at the end, you're
kind of persisting

00:27:13.270 --> 00:27:17.680
that analytical result back
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:27:17.680 --> 00:27:20.870
On the right hand side, we
see semi-long width clusters.

00:27:20.870 --> 00:27:24.040
So this aids in
quite a few things.

00:27:24.040 --> 00:27:25.990
What you see here
illustrated is kind

00:27:25.990 --> 00:27:29.440
of a red, black, or blue
green kind of deployment

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:33.740
where we have version 1.1
of our Hadoop image running.

00:27:33.740 --> 00:27:35.890
But we're also running a
[? Canary ?] for a newer

00:27:35.890 --> 00:27:37.660
version of our platform.

00:27:37.660 --> 00:27:42.340
While it used to be very
hard to upgrade and test

00:27:42.340 --> 00:27:45.490
a new cluster on premises,
like a huge monolithic cluster,

00:27:45.490 --> 00:27:47.840
like, being elastic
in the crowd,

00:27:47.840 --> 00:27:50.740
the ability to spin up
new clusters and basically

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:53.925
load balance between them
makes that a lot easier.

00:27:53.925 --> 00:27:55.300
And then what we
also see here is

00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:59.020
that we can build clusters
for that our purpose built.

00:27:59.020 --> 00:28:03.030
We can have clusters that are
built to run HBase, Druid,

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:07.660
Presto, Spark Streaming, et
cetera, any use case that

00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:11.070
requires long living clusters.

00:28:11.070 --> 00:28:13.510
On top of that, Dataproc
has native integration

00:28:13.510 --> 00:28:18.130
into Cloud Logging and
Google Stackdriver.

00:28:18.130 --> 00:28:22.600
And so you get metrics that the
cluster can use to auto scale.

00:28:22.600 --> 00:28:24.850
You get metrics that
help you understand

00:28:24.850 --> 00:28:27.700
the health of your jobs
and what's going on

00:28:27.700 --> 00:28:29.590
through great operations tools.

00:28:29.590 --> 00:28:32.410
And then lastly, it becomes
very easy to take things

00:28:32.410 --> 00:28:37.210
like Jupiter Lab hosted in Cloud
Datalab or Jupiter Lab hosted

00:28:37.210 --> 00:28:39.760
in Google Cloud Machine
Learning environment,

00:28:39.760 --> 00:28:41.530
and point it at your
Dataproc clusters,

00:28:41.530 --> 00:28:46.010
and use them as an
analytical back end.

00:28:46.010 --> 00:28:48.920
And so we have 3PM Solutions.

00:28:48.920 --> 00:28:52.610
So 3PM is basically
a solution provider

00:28:52.610 --> 00:28:56.120
that basically pulls in
customer reviews and customer

00:28:56.120 --> 00:29:00.380
listings from Amazon, eBay, and
Walmart.com on a daily basis

00:29:00.380 --> 00:29:03.680
and helps sellers as well as
these retail organizations

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:07.610
like, when not understand
that the signal

00:29:07.610 --> 00:29:10.100
from that data and whether
or not some product listings

00:29:10.100 --> 00:29:11.400
are fraudulent.

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:14.960
And they originally had
this solution on premises--

00:29:14.960 --> 00:29:21.830
and it was just unable to
scale due to peaks in demand

00:29:21.830 --> 00:29:31.800
during events like a very
busy fourth quarter retail.

00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:34.310
So what they were trying
to do is they basically

00:29:34.310 --> 00:29:35.490
moved in to the cloud.

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:40.120
They want to spin up clusters
for exactly what they needed.

00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:42.650
They wanted to run with
hundreds and hundred of nodes

00:29:42.650 --> 00:29:45.410
to basically complete this
in a very tight SLA of about

00:29:45.410 --> 00:29:45.980
four hours.

00:29:45.980 --> 00:29:47.840
So what they were able
to do is analyze over

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:51.530
160 million customer
views, ETL that,

00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:54.650
and put that into
an HBase cluster,

00:29:54.650 --> 00:29:58.915
and make that available
for their end users.

00:29:58.915 --> 00:30:00.290
And what's really
kind of amazing

00:30:00.290 --> 00:30:02.913
here is like, when they
built the solution, when

00:30:02.913 --> 00:30:05.330
it was operating at a much
lower scale, that solution just

00:30:05.330 --> 00:30:06.050
scaled with them.

00:30:06.050 --> 00:30:09.980
So the CI/CD and the
solution, et cetera, basically

00:30:09.980 --> 00:30:11.190
scaled with their business.

00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:13.580
And so they didn't need
any additional Hadoop

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:17.420
administrators to kind of scale
that solution as their business

00:30:17.420 --> 00:30:19.980
scale.

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:24.600
Lastly, we have kind of the
lift and transform pattern.

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:27.120
And it's aptly named like,
lifting cloud native.

00:30:27.120 --> 00:30:30.260
This is kind of getting
the best and greatest out

00:30:30.260 --> 00:30:32.550
of Google Cloud Platform.

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:36.030
And so what this is really
kind of trying to show

00:30:36.030 --> 00:30:39.220
is that there's many
injection methods,

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:45.570
whether it be Apache Kafka or
Pub/Sub or external providers

00:30:45.570 --> 00:30:49.340
such as DoubleClick,
YouTube, Adobe Analytics,

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:54.810
et cetera, moving data
into Google Cloud Storage,

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:59.730
Bigtable, et cetera, via
many analytical engines.

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:02.190
We can then bring
any type of analytics

00:31:02.190 --> 00:31:06.570
on top of that, BigQuery,
Dataflow, Dataproc, and start

00:31:06.570 --> 00:31:09.630
doing machine learning
analysis on that data

00:31:09.630 --> 00:31:12.750
to support those use cases,
and then just integrating

00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:15.810
with any I or
visualization tool,

00:31:15.810 --> 00:31:17.700
almost any that's
in the market today,

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:20.280
as well as Google
Cloud Data Studio.

00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:22.020
This is kind of the
lifting cloud needed.

00:31:22.020 --> 00:31:25.320
A couple of points here
since they're kind of new.

00:31:25.320 --> 00:31:27.180
Customers that tend to
lift in cloud native

00:31:27.180 --> 00:31:31.260
tend to do things like
adopt Google Cloud

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:35.430
Dataflow due to its kind
of unified batch and stream

00:31:35.430 --> 00:31:36.090
engine.

00:31:36.090 --> 00:31:39.270
So with very little
modification,

00:31:39.270 --> 00:31:42.065
you can run pipelines both
in batch and streaming.

00:31:42.065 --> 00:31:44.190
While in the Hadoop world,
those were traditionally

00:31:44.190 --> 00:31:46.860
done in different
analytical engines.

00:31:46.860 --> 00:31:49.550
Customers start
to adopt BigQuery.

00:31:49.550 --> 00:31:52.050
We see patterns where
customers will continue

00:31:52.050 --> 00:31:54.870
to do ETL in Hive
or Pig in Hadoop,

00:31:54.870 --> 00:31:58.240
but then look to do
interactive query in BigQuery.

00:31:58.240 --> 00:32:00.570
And then lastly, we
start to see customers

00:32:00.570 --> 00:32:04.050
that go cloud native really
go all in machine learning.

00:32:04.050 --> 00:32:10.320
So as customers become adept
at processing data, managing

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:12.990
its lifecycle, increasing
its discoverability

00:32:12.990 --> 00:32:15.300
through other tools
to its end users,

00:32:15.300 --> 00:32:19.230
we really see that in
a lot of cases, queries

00:32:19.230 --> 00:32:22.890
per user in a company
double six to eight months,

00:32:22.890 --> 00:32:27.180
six to nine months after like,
a large scale adoption of GCP.

00:32:27.180 --> 00:32:29.130
And so we start to
see machine learning

00:32:29.130 --> 00:32:31.770
kind of enter the picture.

00:32:31.770 --> 00:32:34.883
And the company I want to
highlight here is Pandora.

00:32:34.883 --> 00:32:36.300
So we've been
working with Pandora

00:32:36.300 --> 00:32:38.100
for over the last six months.

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:40.590
Pandora had quite
a large on premises

00:32:40.590 --> 00:32:45.300
Hadoop footprint of over 2,700
nodes, 7 petabytes of data.

00:32:45.300 --> 00:32:48.480
And internal analysts
basically participate

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:52.785
in the creation of like,
over 120,000 Hive tables

00:32:52.785 --> 00:32:54.660
that really drove like,
all different corners

00:32:54.660 --> 00:32:55.410
of their business.

00:32:58.370 --> 00:33:02.440
And so if you're looking
to understand how Pandora

00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:05.980
is making this migration
and some of the decisions

00:33:05.980 --> 00:33:07.870
that they made along
the way, there's

00:33:07.870 --> 00:33:09.940
a talk tomorrow afternoon
in the Palace Hotel

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:11.800
as well that talks about
how they move there

00:33:11.800 --> 00:33:14.980
on premise analytics
and interactive query

00:33:14.980 --> 00:33:16.960
to Dataproc and BigQuery.

00:33:16.960 --> 00:33:19.450
And then there's also a
talk on Thursday morning

00:33:19.450 --> 00:33:23.032
on composing Pandora's
move to GCP, which really

00:33:23.032 --> 00:33:25.490
kind of illustrates the journey
for moving from on premises

00:33:25.490 --> 00:33:28.990
Apache Airflow to Google
Cloud Composer for a more

00:33:28.990 --> 00:33:30.205
managed experience.

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:35.960
And so we've reached
the point where

00:33:35.960 --> 00:33:39.620
we understand the tools that
are available within GCP.

00:33:39.620 --> 00:33:43.490
We understand some of the common
patterns that people take.

00:33:43.490 --> 00:33:46.070
And so how do you start
your own migration?

00:33:46.070 --> 00:33:48.650
How do you start
your own journey?

00:33:48.650 --> 00:33:51.023
So first off, it
wasn't quite mentioned,

00:33:51.023 --> 00:33:52.940
but I'm part of Google's
Professional Services

00:33:52.940 --> 00:33:55.430
Organization.

00:33:55.430 --> 00:33:59.950
Google goes above
and beyond to--

00:33:59.950 --> 00:34:03.510
and are obsessed with
customer success.

00:34:03.510 --> 00:34:06.740
So we have a very unconventional
Professional Services

00:34:06.740 --> 00:34:09.590
Organization that's not
a profit center, that

00:34:09.590 --> 00:34:12.350
looks to meet their
customer where they are,

00:34:12.350 --> 00:34:15.989
develops methods and processes
on the fly with our customers

00:34:15.989 --> 00:34:20.480
to basically target and
achieve whatever lift and shift

00:34:20.480 --> 00:34:23.699
pattern that they've chosen.

00:34:23.699 --> 00:34:27.080
And we do that while hoping you
get the most out of your Google

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:28.530
Cloud products.

00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:31.260
And we often work
with partners that--

00:34:31.260 --> 00:34:33.409
kind of Chris
talked to earlier--

00:34:33.409 --> 00:34:35.540
partners that have specific
expertise and who do

00:34:35.540 --> 00:34:39.409
migrations to actually
execute this work.

00:34:42.170 --> 00:34:44.090
So Google Cloud
Professional Services

00:34:44.090 --> 00:34:46.370
has a data analytics
practice within it

00:34:46.370 --> 00:34:49.310
that has migrated
dozens of customers

00:34:49.310 --> 00:34:52.010
from on premises and other
public cloud providers

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:54.440
to GCP through every
single one of the patterns

00:34:54.440 --> 00:34:58.430
that I discussed earlier today.

00:34:58.430 --> 00:35:02.600
And we commonly create
artifacts that we democratize

00:35:02.600 --> 00:35:08.780
via solutions on our website,
blog posts, on the Google Cloud

00:35:08.780 --> 00:35:11.380
data blog, as well as
various articles on medium.

00:35:14.600 --> 00:35:17.035
So we kind of have
over the dozens

00:35:17.035 --> 00:35:18.410
of migrations that
we've actually

00:35:18.410 --> 00:35:21.230
performed, we kind of come up
with an opinionated methodology

00:35:21.230 --> 00:35:25.088
for moving customers,
whether it's

00:35:25.088 --> 00:35:27.005
lift and shift, lift and
re-architect, or lift

00:35:27.005 --> 00:35:28.160
in cloud native.

00:35:28.160 --> 00:35:31.110
It's kind of broken down
into two major themes.

00:35:31.110 --> 00:35:32.360
The first is the plan.

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:34.940
And that is comprised
of four discrete steps.

00:35:34.940 --> 00:35:37.580
And the next is the
deploy, which is six.

00:35:37.580 --> 00:35:42.145
So what typically happens is
over the course of a few weeks

00:35:42.145 --> 00:35:43.520
we lead discovery
workshops where

00:35:43.520 --> 00:35:46.070
we not only understand our
customer's environment,

00:35:46.070 --> 00:35:50.630
but we also educate them on
the really the ins and outs

00:35:50.630 --> 00:35:55.130
of the GCP data stack at
a very, very low level.

00:35:55.130 --> 00:35:58.040
We work with them to determine
end state, an end state that's

00:35:58.040 --> 00:36:01.610
not only a solution
architecture, but also

00:36:01.610 --> 00:36:03.290
organizational changes
that are required

00:36:03.290 --> 00:36:07.820
as well, and in other
ancillary things.

00:36:07.820 --> 00:36:13.700
Next, we work with customers
to prioritize their workloads

00:36:13.700 --> 00:36:16.580
and to actually deep dive
into their data sets,

00:36:16.580 --> 00:36:21.080
and then actually design
the migration path and plan.

00:36:21.080 --> 00:36:24.500
So that kind of wraps
up the planning phase.

00:36:24.500 --> 00:36:26.150
Moving into deployment,
we typically

00:36:26.150 --> 00:36:29.745
see customers immediately
start to move data to GCS.

00:36:29.745 --> 00:36:32.120
When this is typically done
over an interconnect or a VPN

00:36:32.120 --> 00:36:34.037
connection, there's
usually a long tail effort

00:36:34.037 --> 00:36:36.420
to this type of activity.

00:36:36.420 --> 00:36:39.830
And so we kind of get that
kicked off like, pretty quick.

00:36:39.830 --> 00:36:43.730
Next, is we do a deployment
of the big data stacks.

00:36:43.730 --> 00:36:47.240
So we understand web components
are required within the Hadoop

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:48.200
ecosystem.

00:36:48.200 --> 00:36:52.340
And we work to build
an environment that

00:36:52.340 --> 00:36:56.390
is optimized for those
components and working and end.

00:36:56.390 --> 00:36:58.160
Next comes moving the workloads.

00:36:58.160 --> 00:37:02.810
So we actually work to
move every single workload,

00:37:02.810 --> 00:37:05.450
test and validate it,
and at the same time,

00:37:05.450 --> 00:37:08.210
integrate with other GCP
services like Stackdriver,

00:37:08.210 --> 00:37:13.990
Logging, Composer for
Orchestration, BigQuery, et

00:37:13.990 --> 00:37:15.020
cetera.

00:37:15.020 --> 00:37:20.480
And at the end, Google has
Cloud CRE and SRE processes

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:24.530
that kind of help run the system
and make sure that there's

00:37:24.530 --> 00:37:26.248
production operation readiness.

00:37:29.350 --> 00:37:33.510
I just want to cap off
this planning or migration

00:37:33.510 --> 00:37:37.330
section with a sample
Hadoop migration timeline.

00:37:37.330 --> 00:37:40.700
This is customized for every
customer, for every use case.

00:37:40.700 --> 00:37:43.080
And this is kind of typically
the high level activities

00:37:43.080 --> 00:37:45.120
that we kind of see
with the objectives

00:37:45.120 --> 00:37:47.370
that we try to reach.

00:37:47.370 --> 00:37:50.460
So first off, like, we
have analysis kickoff.

00:37:50.460 --> 00:37:53.310
And we understand
the architecture

00:37:53.310 --> 00:37:56.327
of the entire platform
and try to implement

00:37:56.327 --> 00:37:57.160
foundational things.

00:37:57.160 --> 00:38:03.510
So we find that to implement
a big data analyst solution,

00:38:03.510 --> 00:38:06.922
must be operating on kind of
a firm bedrock foundation,

00:38:06.922 --> 00:38:08.880
and those are things like
infrastructure setup.

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:10.505
So you know, it's
very important for us

00:38:10.505 --> 00:38:13.080
to ensure that there is
a correct network design,

00:38:13.080 --> 00:38:19.190
that there is logging, that
their et cetera is in place.

00:38:19.190 --> 00:38:23.970
Kind of flipping the Hadoop
migration on the previous side,

00:38:23.970 --> 00:38:25.620
in another direction
we see here,

00:38:25.620 --> 00:38:28.140
we talk about the data
migration, GCS configuration,

00:38:28.140 --> 00:38:31.410
understanding things like Object
Lifecycle Management, Identity,

00:38:31.410 --> 00:38:33.330
and access management.

00:38:33.330 --> 00:38:35.850
How not only are you going to
have that first initial sync

00:38:35.850 --> 00:38:37.860
of data into GCP,
but how you're going

00:38:37.860 --> 00:38:39.930
to enable the continuous
delivery of data

00:38:39.930 --> 00:38:41.760
that you may be
generating on premises.

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:44.160
So all these things are
kind of taken into account

00:38:44.160 --> 00:38:47.160
and processes are
created, automation

00:38:47.160 --> 00:38:50.320
is created to handle that.

00:38:50.320 --> 00:38:53.670
We find that it's all about
getting that first cluster up

00:38:53.670 --> 00:38:55.650
and going, kind of defining
that data platform.

00:38:55.650 --> 00:38:57.630
Because once you
define it once, you

00:38:57.630 --> 00:38:59.530
can spin it at multiple times.

00:38:59.530 --> 00:39:03.030
And we tend to see two kind of
parallel threads where there's

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:05.440
kind of the migration
of existing workloads

00:39:05.440 --> 00:39:08.290
know after this first
cluster has been created.

00:39:08.290 --> 00:39:10.290
And then at the same time,
other business groups

00:39:10.290 --> 00:39:13.110
within a customer will start
using this new data platform

00:39:13.110 --> 00:39:15.900
to kind of bring new
use cases online.

00:39:15.900 --> 00:39:19.170
And then lastly, there's kind
of the go live preparedness.

00:39:19.170 --> 00:39:21.630
So bringing that production
operational readiness

00:39:21.630 --> 00:39:26.130
through SLA's SLO's, et cetera,
and then foregoing live.

00:39:26.130 --> 00:39:27.820
And then we kind of
rinse and repeat.

00:39:27.820 --> 00:39:30.180
So this is an iterative process.

